AltSql demo: one engine, set up for three devices

1. MACHINE SENSOR  64 KB flash, about 1048 bytes of engine RAM on a 32-bit chip
   A reading a second for an hour. Wi-Fi drops out from minute 20 to 35.
   Gateway A gets every record; gateway B only per-minute summaries and the state.

   05:31  above 60 C for 10 s: alarm raised, fan on
   06:19  back to normal: alarm cleared, fan off
   20:00  Wi-Fi down
   26:59  above 60 C for 10 s: alarm raised, fan on  (Wi-Fi down: decided on the device)
   28:19  back to normal: alarm cleared, fan off
   35:00  Wi-Fi back: sensor catches up, 24128 bytes to gateway A, 702 to gateway B
   48:21  above 60 C for 10 s: alarm raised, fan on
   48:54  back to normal: alarm cleared, fan off

   On the gateways:
    gateway-A> SELECT COUNT(*) AS readings, AVG(temp) AS avg, MAX(temp) AS max FROM raw
      readings    avg         max         
      3600        46.4        68.0        
    gateway-B> SELECT time AS minute, max FROM minute WHERE max > 60
      minute      max         
      5           65.0        
      6           64.7        
      26          67.3        
      27          68.0        
      28          68.0        
      48          65.0        
    gateway-B> SELECT key, value FROM kv
      key         value       
      fan         off         
   Gateway A holds all 3600 readings, including the 900 taken while Wi-Fi was down.

2. COLD-CHAIN LOGGER  512 KB flash in 64 KB blocks, about 920 bytes of engine RAM
   A reading every 30 s for a 48-hour run. Mobile coverage only at depots.
   Over the air: 15-minute summaries and excursion records. Full log read out at the dock.

   16h00  depot: coverage back, 2142 bytes sent over the mobile network
   22h42  above 8 C for 15 min: excursion recorded, driver alerted  (no coverage: decided on the logger)
   23h18  back below 8 C: excursion of 51 min closed, peak 12.4 C
   32h00  depot: coverage back, 2233 bytes sent over the mobile network
   47h30  depot: coverage back, 2074 bytes sent over the mobile network

   In the cloud, from the mobile link:
    cloud> SELECT time / 3600 AS start_hour, minutes, peak FROM excursion
      start_hour  minutes     peak        
      22          51          12.4        
    cloud> SELECT COUNT(*) AS quarters, MAX(max) AS warmest FROM quarter WHERE max > 8
      quarters    warmest     
      13          12.4        
   At the dock, from the full read-out:
    dock> SELECT COUNT(*) AS readings, SUM(door) AS door_open, MIN(temp) AS coldest, MAX(temp) AS warmest FROM raw
      readings    door_open   coldest     warmest     
      5760        32          3.7         12.4        
   Door openings warmed the load past 8 C for a few minutes without counting as excursions.

3. SOIL SENSOR  32 KB flash, about 672 bytes of engine RAM
   A reading every 15 minutes for 30 days. The sensor runs the irrigation valve itself.
   Radio: one message a day of at most 51 bytes, carrying the daily summary.

   day 10  moisture 19.9% for an hour: valve opened
   day 10  moisture back to 35.8%: valve closed
   day 14  moisture 19.9% for an hour: valve opened
   day 14  moisture back to 35.4%: valve closed
   day 18  moisture 19.9% for an hour: valve opened
   day 19  moisture back to 35.8%: valve closed
   day 30  moisture 20.0% for an hour: valve opened
   day 30  moisture back to 36.2%: valve closed

   At the farm office, from 30 radio messages:
    farm-office> SELECT COUNT(*) AS days, MIN(min) AS driest, SUM(irrigation) AS irrigation_minutes FROM day
      days        driest      irrigation_minutes
      30          19.7        780         
    farm-office> SELECT time / 86400 + 1 AS day, avg, irrigation FROM day WHERE irrigation > 0
      day         avg         irrigation  
      10          30.5        195         
      14          24.2        195         
      18          21.8        120         
      19          36.0        75          
      30          31.7        195         
   The sensor keeps the most recent days of 15-minute detail; its 32 KB rolled over 2251 older readings.

Same engine, three set-ups
                            Machine sensor          Cold-chain logger       Soil sensor             
  flash on the device       64 KB                   512 KB                  32 KB                   
  engine RAM (32-bit chip)  about 1048 bytes        about 920 bytes         about 672 bytes         
  readings                  1 per second, 1 hour    1 per 30 s, 48 hours    1 per 15 min, 30 days   
  decided on the device     overheat: alarm, fan    excursion record        irrigation valve        
  link                      Wi-Fi, 15-min outage    mobile, at depots only  radio, 51 bytes a day   
  sent upstream             2533 bytes              6810 bytes              1140 bytes              
  every reading as records  96133 bytes             179610 bytes            86400 bytes             
  every reading as JSON     190800 bytes            328374 bytes            207360 bytes            
  flash written on device   104204 bytes            191392 bytes            94820 bytes             
  sector erases             25 (1.6 per sector)     2 (0.2 per sector)      23 (2.9 per sector)     

